  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nDry through the CWA early this morning with a front with little\nto no precip along it stalled to our north.  Through the day, \nPWATs continue to climb above 2\" for the entire area. A \nshortwave is forecast to pass west of ENC, but should provide \nenough of a glancing blow combined with the seabreeze and an \ninland trough to support scattered convection capable of very \nheavy rainfall rates and gusty winds, particularly along and\nwest of hwy 17. The increased southerly flow should lead to a \nmore progressive seabreeze which should also favor a more \nprogressive nature to the convection. While this would limit the\nduration of heavy rainfall rates over any one location, the \nrates could be high enough to support minor hydro concerns, \nespecially in urban areas. Of note, despite anomalous moisture \nand moderate instability, convective coverage on Saturday is \nless certain as the strongest forcing is expected to be outside \nof ENC. Despite moderate instability, weak shear and lower DCAPE\nshould help to keep the severe thunderstorm risk at a minimum.\n\nOn Sunday, guidance continues the trend of slowing down the \narrival of a cold front that will be dropping south out of \nVirginia. The front now looks to come through Sunday night. This\npoints towards an extra day of moist and unstable conditions, \nwith another round of scattered to perhaps numerous \nthunderstorms. Deep layer shear is forecast to slightly \nincrease, but the overall severe thunderstorm risk continues to\nlook low. With anomalous PWATs in place, heavy rainfall rates \ncan be expected, with a continued risk of minor flooding \nimpacts.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A much drier airmass is forecast to build in \nacross the Eastern U.S. early next week in the wake of Sunday \nnight's cold front. Additionally, guidance have trended a bit \ncooler within the developing northeasterly flow regime, offering\na reprieve from the higher humidity over this weekend. From \nmid-week on, guidance differ on whether it warms up or stays \nmilder. Additionally, guidance suggests a decent coastal trough \nmay develop along what is leftover from this weekend's front. \nWith ridging building in, this trough may slide west beneath the\nridge and bring a return to unsettled conditions, especially \nalong the coast by Thursday.",
